    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fb57d3bd-2c64-56db-8035-e0a1d24eddad",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i}+kR_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below $a_{11}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{2}:=R_{2}-2R_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{3}:=R_{3}+3R_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 17\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$R_{2}:=R_{2}-2R_{1}$ and $R_{3}:=R_{3}+3R_{1}$.",
